.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt NDWATCH 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndwatch
.Nd follow the DNS servers and DHCPv6 flags of an interface's routers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar interface
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
follows the router advertisements of
.Ar interface
for what the kernel leaves to a program, and stays in the foreground.
.Xr init 8
runs it for
.Ql eth0
where the boot line hands the network to userland, and starts it again
when it ends.
.Pp
The kernel passes each RDNSS and DNSSL option of an advertisement
(RFC 8106) to netlink as
.Dv RTM_NEWNDUSEROPT
.Pq Xr rtnetlink 7 .
.Nm
keeps up to four servers and six search domains with their lifetimes,
removes one whose lifetime ends or that an option gives with lifetime 0,
and gives them to the resolver merge each time they change.
A link-local server is given with its interface, as
.Ql fe80::1%eth0 .
A domain is taken only when it has nothing but letters, digits,
.Ql -
and
.Ql _ .
.Pp
It reads the M and O flags from the advertisements themselves, on a raw
ICMPv6 socket whose filter passes only router advertisements from a
link-local source.
The M flag starts
.Xr udhcpc6 1
for an address and servers, and the O flag alone starts it with
.Fl l ,
for servers alone; a client for servers that an M flag finds is replaced
by one for an address.
A client that an
.Ic up
line of an
.Ic inet6
stanza started, whose pid is in
.Pa /var/run/udhcpc6. Ns Ar interface Ns Pa .pid ,
is left alone.
.Pp
When the link goes down, or the interface does,
.Nm
stops the client it started, which gives back its lease and takes its
address and servers away, and forgets every server and domain, since the
network that comes back may be another.
It sends one router solicitation when it starts, since the kernel's were
answered before it was listening, and again when a full netlink queue
lost messages.
On
.Dv SIGTERM
it stops its client and takes its servers out of the resolver's file.
.Ss The resolver merge
.Pa /usr/share/udhcpc/resolv.script Ar name
writes
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf
from what each source of servers gives.
It reads the source's lines,
.Ql nameserver Ar address
and
.Ql search Ar domain ... ,
on its standard input and keeps them in
.Pa /var/run/resolv/ Ns Ar name ,
or removes that file for empty input, then writes the resolver's file
whole from every file there in the order of their names, each server and
domain once.
The IPv4 lease's script gives
.Ar interface Ns Pa .dhcp ,
the DHCPv6 lease's
.Ar interface Ns Pa .dhcp6
and
.Nm
.Ar interface Ns Pa .ra ,
so the IPv4 servers come first and the routers' last, and no source drops
another's.
A source that never gave anything and gives nothing changes nothing, so
the file the image starts with stands until a source has a server.
The merge holds
.Xr flock 2
on
.Pa /var/run/resolv/.lock
and writes through a temporary file beside the resolver's.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /usr/share/udhcpc/resolv.script
The resolver merge.
.It Pa /var/run/resolv/
The servers and domains of each source.
.It Pa /var/run/udhcpc6. Ns Ar interface Ns Pa .pid
The pid of the interface's DHCPv6 client.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Messages go to standard error, which init sends to
.Pa /var/log/services .
.Nm
exits 2 for a wrong argument and 1 when the interface does not exist or a
socket cannot be opened.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr ipv6 7 ,
.Xr netlink 7 ,
.Xr rtnetlink 7 ,
.Xr init 8
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 4861, neighbour discovery; RFC 8106, the RDNSS and DNSSL options.
